## 3. Scripting Layer (Lua)/ Runtime

### Milestone 3.1 — Lua-to-Godot API surface
- [X] Port the existing xLua-based C# API surface (position, color, spawn, etc.) from the Unity prototype to Godot's C# host
- [x] LuaManager-equivalent singleton for Godot
- [x] Re-verify the "script moves/recolors an object" proof-of-concept in the Godot scene graph
**Acceptance criteria:** A Lua script attached to an object can read/set its position and color, matching the working Unity prototype's behavior.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 3.2 — Unified per-entity script model
- [x] One Lua script per entity, networking expressed through namespaced `net.server` / `net.client` tables instead of manually instantiated remote events
- [x] Implicit RPC routing — ordinary function declarations under `net.server` / `net.client` become remote calls, with no manual event binding, string identifiers, or client/server if-guards in creator code
- [x] Strict server authority — `net.server` functions execute only on the host; client calls transmit to the host, host calls broadcast to connected clients
- [x] Metatable hooks trap both declaration and call, implemented as sugar over a single bound C# API method (never direct Godot access from Lua — §1.1 boundary)
- [x] The lot root is itself an entity: its script runs at lot load, so a new lot can spawn its capsule character and camera in Lua *(the load-time root-script path exists; the capsule/camera content itself lands with §3.4)*
- [x] Graceful offline degradation — `net.*` resolves to direct local calls when a lot runs with no network (single-player, editor Test mode — see §3.4)
- [x] Argument serialization rules defined (which Lua types cross the wire; error behaviour for unsupported types)
- [x] Design doc before code: peer model (the host peer, not a hardcoded peer ID, given the §7.2 P2P hosting path), behaviour when a client calls an undefined `net.server` function, and whether one script may declare both `net.server` and `net.client`
**Implementation status:** complete for the offline/loopback path (real P2P transport is §7, behind an `INetTransport` seam). Verified by `NetSelfTest` (169 fast checks on private Lua states, plus an opt-in 8-check heavy set via `OPENLOT_HEAVY_TESTS`) + `GizmoSelfTest` (171 checks). Index: see the **Implementation Index** section near the end of this file, and `docs/design/milestone-3.2-unified-script-model.md`.
**Acceptance criteria:** Two entities with separate scripts interact as plain function calls — a client invoking `net.server` performs a host-authoritative action, and the host invoking `net.client` triggers local client behaviour such as an animation or audio cue — with no creator code containing manual remote-event setup or client/server branching, and a new lot spawning its default character without manual scripting.
**Difficulty:** 🔴 (one unified model is a single design problem — the routing design must be settled before implementation)

### Milestone 3.3 — Godot renderer baseline
- [x] Skybox rendering (procedural sky + shadowed directional sun, `BuilderScene.BuildViewport`)
- [x] Scene graph / object model for lots (LotRoot / LotUIRoot containers + integer handle registry — OpenLot-specific, not Godot's default scene tree semantics)
**Acceptance criteria:** A lot loads with a skybox, a lit scene with visible directional shadows, and a scene-graph object model the Lua API can address by handle, all inside the Godot host.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 

### Milestone 3.4 — Test mode / Game mode + capsule character
*Test mode and the capsule character are one milestone: Test mode is not meaningfully playable without a controllable character, and the character is a runtime concern rather than an art/asset one (it used to live in §5 as "capsule prototyping").*

*Capsule work*
- [x] Capsule character controller with basic movement and orbital camera (`CapsuleController` + `OrbitalCamera`: kinematic `CharacterBody3D` swept with `MoveAndSlide`, camera-relative WASD, gravity/jump, floor detection; the body excludes the part's own static collider, the same technique `PartDragController` uses)
- [x] A brand-new lot ships with base default Lua code that spawns the capsule character and camera, so it is playable immediately on creation with no manual scripting (`BuilderScene.EnsureDefaultLotScript` writes/attaches `user://Scripts/Character.lua`, which spawns the capsule at lot load; the camera is created by the runtime on session entry — scripts cannot touch Godot nodes, §1.1 — and `FindCharacter()` picks the first Capsule as the player)

*Mode switching*
- [x] Activate the two stubbed builder menu entries (`Builder.DrawMainMenuBar`: Test → Play in Test Mode / Stop Test Mode, Game → Play in Game Mode / Stop Game Mode; Escape leaves a session)
- [x] Test mode — run the lot in place from the creation environment: freecam hands off to the player camera and controller, scripts execute, and the creator can drop back into edit mode without losing state (editor tooling suspended, lot UI still drawn)
- [x] Game mode — the runtime player session a lot is played in, with no creation-environment tooling active (same session with the dockspace, menu bar and panels not drawn; the 3D view fills the display)

*Known implementation seams (from the pre-3.4 code review — resolve these, don't rediscover them)*
- [x] `LotObjectKind` gains a Capsule kind and `LotLuaApi` a matching bound spawn method (`LotObjectKind` was Cube/Sphere/Cylinder/Plane; the bound surface had no capsule spawn). Documented next to the binding per §2.2. Landed with sandbox parity: a `capsule()` convenience wrapper in both the entity sandbox (`LuaBootstrap`) and the scratch chunk (`LuaManager`), plus a Toolbox spawn button. Verified by `NetIntegrationTest.TestLiveCapsuleSpawnPath` (real VM: reflection registration → sandbox proxy → mesh + collision build → reload cleanup)
- [x] Camera ownership handoff: `FreecamController` hardcoded `Current = true` at build time and gated input on a layout-time `ViewportWindow` flag. Extracted `ICameraInputSource` (`CameraInputSource.cs`): the Viewport window resolves the per-frame conditions once and both cameras read from it, each acting only while Godot-`Current` and only for its own session
- [x] `ViewportWindow.InputEnabled` is an instance property reassigned from modal state every layout pass (`Builder.DrawLayout`) — the Test-mode gate composes with that assignment via the pure `ViewportWindow.ResolveCameraAuthority(inputEnabled, focused, playerSession, ...)`: a modal denies both cameras, the session then decides which one gets the rest (verified by 4 integration checks)
- [x] Script runtime lifecycle is start/stop-able: `BuilderScene.StartScriptRuntime()` / `StopScriptRuntime()` replaced the one-shot `_Ready`/`_ExitTree` block, so the runtime can be restarted mid-session (verified: stop clears it, start returns a running runtime that re-runs the lot's scripts). Reload-on-transition is the intended shape (see decision below); wiring this pair into the Test-mode menu entries is the remaining half
- [ ] Player-originated `net.*` attribution wired up: `LuaNetBridge.SimulatePlayerInteraction` exists but only the self-tests call it. **Blocked — there is no input→script call path to scope yet**: lot UI elements have no click handling (`LotUIElement` is visual-only; interaction is §4.2), movement/look never cross into Lua, and the "button → door" self-test drives its handler directly rather than through player input. The first real consumer is lot-UI interaction, so this lands with it rather than behind fabricated plumbing
- [x] Debug physics smoke test (`BuilderScene.RunPhysicsSmokeTest`) spawned bodies into the lot physics space — now skipped while a session is active. (The default character is also spawned clear of the test's origin-region probes: a ray straight down at x=0 asserts exact contact geometry, and a capsule parked at the origin broke 8 of its assertions)
- [x] `PartDragController` runs in `Builder._PhysicsProcess` and never fights the character for a body: entering a session queues the drag's release, and the Viewport window skips pointer input entirely during a session, so no drag can start or survive

*Decisions settled for this milestone*
- **Entering Test mode reloads the lot** (script runtime restarts, `start()` re-runs, script-owned spawns re-spawn); exiting tears it down again and returns to the build-mode world. Chosen over an in-place transition because it is the honest runtime semantics, matches what the §3.2 design doc already describes, and avoids making the editor/runtime boundary re-entrant. Cost: unsaved Inspector edits do not survive a Test-mode round trip — acceptable while §8 save/load does not exist, since nothing else is durable yet
- **State ownership:** script-owned spawns are destroyed on reload, so a capsule spawned by a lot-root script is re-spawned on re-entry rather than preserved across it
- **`net.*` in Test mode** resolves through the existing `LoopbackTransport` — there is no peer, so calls stay local; only the origination scope changes

**Implementation status:** landed and verified headless. `ICameraInputSource` + `OrbitalCamera` + `CapsuleController` are new; `Builder.EnterTestMode`/`ExitTestMode` and the Test/Game menu entries drive `BuilderScene.EnterTestMode(bool gameMode)`. Verified by `NetIntegrationTest.TestPlayerSessionCameraHandoff` (15 checks: the gate-composition truth table, the camera handoff both ways, character attach/detach, Test vs. Game) plus `TestLiveCapsuleSpawnPath` (6 checks) — 59 integration checks green alongside `GizmoSelfTest` 171 and `NetSelfTest` 169. One seam left open and marked blocked below (Player-originated `net.*`).
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator can hit Test mode from the builder and immediately play their lot — a brand-new empty lot spawns a controllable capsule character with a working camera and running scripts, with no manual scripting — then return to editing; Game mode plays the same lot with no editor tooling present.
**Difficulty:** 🔴 (two coupled halves plus a new runtime/editor boundary — the mode switch, the character, and the script lifecycle have to land together or not at all)

### Milestone 3.5 — Collision groups and layers
- [ ] Per-object collision layer/mask editing (currently hardcoded `CollisionLayer = 1` / `CollisionMask = 0` on every part) with named groups
- [ ] Which interactions collide is creator-controlled; the host stays authoritative for anything with enforcement impact (§1.3)
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator puts objects into named collision groups and only the intended objects interact.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 3.6 — Constraints and joints
- [ ] Hinge and weld constraints between parts, requiring dynamic bodies (parts are static-only today)
- [ ] Decide how constraints interact with Anchored / CanCollide from §2.3
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator hinges or welds two parts and they hold or pivot correctly in play.
**Difficulty:** 🔴 (needs a dynamic-body layer first — pairs with the Anchored/CanCollide semantics decision in §2.3)

### Milestone 3.7 — Event/signal system
- [ ] Script-subscribed events: `touched`, `clicked`, plus per-frame and timer callbacks — scripts today have only load + `start()`, with `Tick()` never reaching Lua
- [ ] Event registration through the bound API (narrow subscribe/unsubscribe methods per §1.1, never raw Godot signal connections leaking into Lua)
- [ ] Handlers run under the existing watchdog/memory budgets; one misbehaving handler must not stall the frame (frame-budget accounting in `ScriptRuntime.Tick`)
- [ ] `touched`/`clicked` carry the originating player through the §3.4 attribution path (`BeginPlayerInteraction`), so this lands together with the in-lot command UI (4.2) and lot UI activation (4.3) — one shared in-session input blocker
- [ ] `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` wait on §7.1 (no peers exist on `LoopbackTransport`); the chat UI work in 7.3 can use them once they land
**Acceptance criteria:** A part's script reacts to touch and click, a timer callback fires on schedule without tripping the watchdog, and a player-triggered handler executes with player attribution.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 event registration + budgets · 🔴 player attribution (blocked with 3.4/4.2/4.3) · `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` blocked on 7.1

### Milestone 3.8 — Tween/timing library
- [ ] Smooth property changes from Lua (position, rotation, color, UI rects) without per-frame loops in scripts
- [ ] Implemented as a C# tween list advanced in `ScriptRuntime.Tick`, writing through the existing Lua-accessible setters — Lua never runs per frame, so the watchdog budgets are untouched
- [ ] Delayed/repeating timers with cancel handles, surfaced through the same tick rather than Godot's `SceneTreeTimer` (keeps determinism across the reload-on-Test-mode boundary from 3.4)
**Acceptance criteria:** A script tweens a part across the lot and schedules a delayed callback with one call each, with no per-frame Lua and no watchdog trips.
**Difficulty:** 🟡/🟠 (a C# list plus tick integration; the design constraint is the watchdog, not the math)

### Milestone 3.9 — Input API
- [ ] Key/mouse/gamepad state readable from scripts, polled once per frame and cached (not one Lua↔C# call per key — the boundary-crossing cost the perf rules flag)
- [ ] Composes with §4.5 keybinding customization and §12.1 gamepad support: scripts see logical actions, never hardcoded physical keys (today `CapsuleController`/`FreecamController` hardcode WASD)
- [ ] Authority rule documented: raw input is client-local; anything with enforcement impact goes through host-authoritative `net.server` (§1.3) — decided here, not later
**Acceptance criteria:** A script reads jump/interact actions each frame via logical names, works with rebound keys and a gamepad, and a hostile client cannot move another player's character.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 (+ 🔴 if input ever drives enforcement — see §1.3)

### Milestone 3.10 — Camera API
- [ ] Script-selectable modes: first-person, fixed, and scripted (cutscene) — built as modes on the existing freecam/orbital machinery (`OrbitalCamera` seeding + the shared-input handoff already exist), not a second camera stack
- [ ] Cutscene scripting writes through per-frame C# interpolation (same tick pattern as 3.8), so Lua describes the shot and C# moves the camera
- [ ] Handoff rule with Test-mode entry/exit (3.4) and character attach/detach documented — which owner holds the camera in each state
**Acceptance criteria:** A script switches a player to first-person, pins a fixed camera, then plays a scripted cutscene move and returns control cleanly.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 3.11 — Physics API
- [ ] Raycasts from Lua (the query path already exists internally in `PartDragController` — this is exposure, not new physics)
- [ ] Forces/velocity on parts — **depends on the dynamic-body layer from §3.6** (parts are static-only today); one dependency, shared with constraints/joints
- [ ] Material properties per part (friction, bounciness, mass) once bodies exist; names reserved in the 2.3 property declaration table meanwhile so the Inspector and Lua agree on vocabulary
**Acceptance criteria:** A script raycasts to find what's ahead, pushes a dynamic part with a force, and sets friction/bounciness that observably change how it slides and bounces.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 raycasts · 🔴 forces/velocity (blocked on 3.6's dynamic bodies) · 🟡 material properties once bodies exist

### Milestone 3.12 — Per-player persistent data API (DataStore equivalent)
- [ ] Design doc before code (same bar as 3.2): where the data lives under P2P hosting (§7.2) — host-held with client cache vs. each-peer-owns-own — settled before any storage code
- [ ] Key/value per-player store with read/write through the bound API; enforcement-relevant reads stay host-authoritative (§1.3)
- [ ] On-disk slot reserved in the §8.1 `.lot` format for player data, decided together with the format rather than bolted on later
**Acceptance criteria:** A script saves a player's coins, the value survives a lot reload, and the ownership model is documented — no silent "client tells the host its balance" trust hole.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 storage · 🔴 ownership design (decide early)

---

## 4. Terminal / Menu UI

*Status: functionally complete from the Unity prototype; migration to Godot in progress (main menu + terminal already moved and fixed up as of today).*

### Milestone 4.1 — terminal system part 2
- [ ] Re-verify command set parity: help, openlot (join/host/list/create), clear, neofetch, sysinfo, motd, ping, matrix, register/login/logout, theme, alias, ls, avatar, disconnect, registertopublic
- [ ] Re-verify auth gate (login/register/help/clear open pre-login, everything else locked)
- [ ] Re-verify "openlot list" interactive TUI selection flow into a lot
**Acceptance criteria:** Every command from the Unity terminal build works identically in the Godot port, auth gating behaves the same, and TUI list navigation loads a selected lot.
**Difficulty:** 🟩/🟠 (Rolkka-owned, feel/fit-and-finish)

### Milestone 4.2 — In-lot command UI
- [ ] Design and build an in-game window/UI system for commands while inside a lot (the old TerminalOutput UI was removed from LocalLot for being buggy there — needs a proper replacement, not a reuse)
**Acceptance criteria:** A player/creator can open a command UI while inside a live lot without the bugs that broke the previous in-lot terminal attempt.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

### Milestone 4.3 — Lot UI customization
- [ ] More customization options for lot UI elements (`LotUIRoot`), using the same typed property widgets as 2.3
- [ ] Checkbox and dropdown controls available as lot UI elements, not only in the editor (new `LotUIKind`s — today it is Frame/Button/Text/Scrollbar)
- [ ] Scrollable containers for lot UI, using ImGui's existing child-region support (`BeginChild`) for clipping and scrolling rather than the current `Scrollbar` kind, which draws a static thumb over nothing
- [ ] Click routing that reaches script handlers: element presses resolved through ImGui item state inside the viewport window, extended past today's editor-only selection/drag path in `UiGizmoController` to actual activation during a player session
- [ ] Customization properties settable from Lua
*Note: the click-routing and scroll-container items share the blocker with the in-lot command UI in 4.2 and the player-originated `net.*` attribution item in 3.4 — the ImGui primitives exist, but there is no in-session path from a lot UI press to a script handler yet.*
**Acceptance criteria:** A creator can build a lot UI panel with a checkbox, a dropdown and a scroll area, style it, and have a script react to its values.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 (ImGui supplies scrolling and item-state hit-testing; the work is element kinds, property wiring, and extending routing to in-session activation)

### Milestone 4.4 — Client settings system
- [ ] Settings screen grown from the existing theme-only window (`Terminal.DrawSettingsWindow`) into a real, tabbed settings surface
- [ ] Camera settings: FOV, mouse sensitivity (already fields on `FreecamController` / `OrbitalCamera`, currently hardcoded to 0.15), invert-Y, and a scope decision (per-camera or global)
- [ ] Graphics settings: resolution scale / viewport size, shadow toggle, MSAA, render method — applied live where Godot allows it
- [ ] Settings persisted to disk and applied on startup, with a restore-defaults action
- [ ] Settings reachable in-lot as well as from the main menu, so a player can change them without leaving a session
**Acceptance criteria:** A player can open settings from the main menu or in-lot, change FOV/sensitivity/graphics options, see them take effect immediately, and find them still applied after restarting the client.
**Difficulty:** 🟠 FOV/sensitivity/persistence · 🟠 graphics options · 🟠 in-lot access

### Milestone 4.5 — Keybinding customization
- [ ] Rebindable keys for editor tools, camera and player controls, with conflict detection and a restore-defaults action (feeds §4.4's settings screen)
**Acceptance criteria:** A player rebinds a key, and both the editor and in-lot controls use the new binding after a restart.
**Difficulty:** 🟠

