## Implementation Index — Milestone 3.2 (`net.*` + per-entity scripts)

Full decision record, execution flow and limitations: **`docs/design/milestone-3.2-unified-script-model.md`**.
Creator-facing reference: **`docs/net-api.md`**.

**Where the pieces live** (all under `builder/`):

| Piece | File | One-line contract |
|---|---|---|
| VM bootstrap + sandbox | `LuaBootstrap.cs` | The Lua chunk: default-deny whitelist, sealed metatables, `makeNet(handle)`, validation pre-pass, watchdog hook, env loader, cross-entity sugar. Everything here is sugar over the bound `LotLuaApi` methods — no Godot access from Lua (§1.1). |
| Watchdog | `LuaWatchdog.cs` | Instruction budgets (200k/dispatch, 1M/script load, 4M/frame, 3 trips ⇒ circuit breaker). Hook installed **from Lua**; always cleared **from C#** first. |
| Memory guard | `LuaMemoryGuard.cs` | `lua_setallocf` hard cap (64 MB), `OomHit` flag checked after every dispatch, VM rebuilt at a frame boundary. |
| Net bridge | `LuaNetBridge.cs` | `IDispatchTarget`: decode args onto the raw Lua stack, dispatch, trip counting, script-name registry, OOM/deferred rebuild, script loading. |
| Router | `NetRouter.cs` + `NetTransport.cs` | Transport-agnostic envelope/queue/rate-limit/authority core; `INetTransport` is the §7 seam (`LoopbackTransport` today). |
| Wire codec | `NetSerializer.cs` | Argument tags, table array/pair sections, all budgets, stack-based decode, wrapper disposal. |
| Script runtime | `ScriptRuntime.cs` | Script discovery, per-script envs, deterministic handle order, lot-root-at-load, frame `Tick`, teardown. |
| Lifecycle | `LuaManager.cs`, `BuilderScene.cs` | VM lifecycle/scratch path (`RunString`) and the lot world: handle registry (root = 0), load-order handles, script load via `StartScriptRuntime` (restartable — the §3.4 Test-mode re-entry seam), `_Process` tick, `StopScriptRuntime` in `_ExitTree` teardown order. |
| Verification | `NetSelfTest.cs` + `NetIntegrationTest.cs` | 169 unit checks (private Lua states only) + an opt-in 8-check heavy set (`OPENLOT_HEAVY_TESTS`) + 59 integration checks driving the REAL LuaManager/ScriptRuntime and live lot; all run headless under `#if DEBUG`. The fast set prints its wall-clock; integration waits a few physics frames, prints a "suite complete: N checks" line and exits non-zero if it aborts. |

**Invariants any future change must keep**
- Lua reaches C# only through `LotLuaApi`; `NetInvoke`/`NetRegister`/`CallServer`/`CallClient` are the only net entry points, and the raw table-taking forms stay out of creator reach.
- All net traffic is queued and dispatched from `NetRouter.Flush()`; nothing executes synchronously, and recursion must never become stack depth.
- Contract changes to bound methods, tag bytes, table sections or creator-visible names are breaking changes to creator-facing API — call them out, never slip them in.
- The watchdog hook is installed from Lua (error() is safe there) and cleared from C# after every unit; never throw managed exceptions through native Lua frames.

**Known limitations (see the design doc §6 for detail)**
- Scripts run at lot load only — hot re-attaching a script waits for §3.4 Test mode.
- Editor-spawned objects get handles only once a registration path touches them (option A covers everything present at load).
- `coroutine` is unavailable in scripts; single blocking built-ins cannot be preempted.
- Numbers cross as doubles (2^53 precision); an OOM rebuild loses script globals.
- **Native `lua54` is not packaged yet** — Lua runs only with a compatible `liblua54.so` on the library path; bundling it is required before Test mode ships.
- Load-time spawns are owned by their script and removed before a reload (F2 closed); handler spawns are unowned by design. Physics smoke-test timing sensitivity (F4, deferred). Scripting suspension is terminal until the lot reloads and shows as a red banner in the Viewport window.
- Origination rule: autonomous calls (lot load, start(), timers) are System in ALL modes; Player only inside an explicit interaction scope (`NetRouter.BeginPlayerInteraction`; Test mode: `SimulatePlayerInteraction`).
---

## Sequencing Notes

- **MLP/Alpha (target Mid-late Nov 2026):** §1 Migration/terminal, §2 Creation Environment (Build Mode: 2.3 Inspector properties, 2.4 editor workflow, 2.5 lighting/environment), §3 Scripting/Runtime (§3.4 Test/Game mode + capsule character, §3.5 collision groups, §3.6 constraints/joints, §3.7 events/signals, §3.8 tween/timing, §3.9 input, §3.10 camera, §3.11 physics, §3.12 per-player data), §4 Terminal/Menu (§4.3 lot UI customization, §4.4 client settings, §4.5 keybindings), §7.3 chatting (bubbles/UI only), §8 Save/load, §9 Testing loop (§9.3 in-client reporting). This is the playable, buildable core — no monetization, no providers, no plugins yet.
  - **Carve-outs inside the MLP set:** private P2P chat (7.3) and `playerJoined`/`playerLeft` (3.7) wait on §7.1's transport; autosave/crash recovery (2.4), environment persistence (2.5) and the player-data slot (3.12) are blocked on §8.1's `.lot` format; forces/velocity and material properties (3.11) and constraints/joints (3.6) wait on a dynamic-body layer that does not exist yet. Build the rest, land these as the dependency arrives.
- **Post-MLP — Official Plugins (§11.4):** Ko-fi/Patreon first (cheapest, no custody issue), Roblox compatibility research started early even though shipping is later (it's the linchpin of the developer-migration growth thesis), Bender and others after.
- **Post-MLP — Other Features:** §5.1 rigged characters, §6.1 animation system + §6.2 audio, §7.1–7.2 full networking, §10 full plugin tiers, §11.1–11.3 provider system and monetization, §12.1 controller support, §12.2 template lots, §12.3 localization (to be decided), §12.6 asset browser, §12.7 lot snapshots, §12.8 spawn points/teams/checkpoints, §12.9 leaderboard, §12.10 creator credits/license metadata, §12.11 accessibility.
- **Ongoing:** Polish pass driven by real MLP usage data rather than pre-guessed priorities.
